        #[
                version: 0.3.0
                date: to :date "2026-08-05T17:54:48+00:00"
                link: "https://github.com/Lulzx/schema.art/releases/tag/v0.3.0"
                details: #[
                        size: 85328
                        files: 19
                        readme: {
                                <div align="center">
                                
                                <img src="docs/logo.svg" width="140" alt="schema"/>
                                
                                # schema
                                
                                ### Say what a value must be. Get back every field that disagrees.
                                
                                [![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-555555.svg?style=for-the-badge)](LICENSE) [![Language](https://img.shields.io/badge/language-Arturo-6A7FC8.svg?style=for-the-badge)](https://arturo-lang.io)
                                
                                </div>
                                
                                ---
                                
                                A schema is an ordinary Arturo dictionary. Constraints are ordinary blocks. Nothing here is a macro or a special form. The dialect is data that `check` knows how to read.
                                
                                ```red
                                import "schema"!
                                
                                email: {/[a-z0-9._]+@[a-z0-9]+\.[a-z]{2,}/}
                                
                                address: #[
                                    street: [:string min: 3]
                                    city:   [:string]
                                ]
                                
                                user: #[
                                    name: [:string between: 2..64]
                                    mail: [:string matches: email]
                                    age:  [:integer opt min: 18]
                                    role: [:string in: ["admin" "editor" "viewer"]]
                                    tags: [:block opt of: [:string min: 1]]
                                    home: [:dictionary of: address]
                                ]
                                
                                check? user #[name: "Ada" mail: "ada@example.com" role: "admin"
                                              home: #[street: "Main" city: "AKL"]]
                                ; => true
                                ```
                                
                                Where it fails, it says where and why:
                                
                                ```red
                                print explain check user #[name: "A" mail: "nope" role: "wizard"
                                                           home: #[street: "X"] extra: 1]
                                ```
                                
                                ```
                                - name should be within 2..64
                                - mail should match email
                                - role should be one of [admin editor viewer]
                                - home.street should be at least 3
                                - home.city is required
                                - extra is not a known field
                                ```
                                
                                ## Install
                                
                                ```sh
                                arturo -p install schema
                                ```
                                
                                Then, from anywhere:
                                
                                ```red
                                import "schema"!
                                ```
                                
                                Arturo 0.10.0 or newer, and nothing else.
                                
                                ## Interface
                                
                                | | |
                                |---|---|
                                | `check schema value` | block of faults; an empty block means valid |
                                | `check? schema value` | logical |
                                | `coerce schema value` | a transformed copy; never fails, never judges |
                                | `conform schema value` | the coerced value, or `null` if it won't do |
                                | `example schema` | a value the schema accepts |
                                | `explain faults` | one line per fault, for humans |
                                
                                `check`, `check?` and `conform` all take `.open`, which tolerates keys the schema does not mention, at every level of the value rather than only at the root.
                                
                                A fault is a plain dictionary, so you can group, filter or reshape it however you like:
                                
                                ```red
                                #[path: ["home" "city"]  code: 'missing]
                                #[path: ["tags" 1]       code: 'tooSmall  expected: 1  got: ""]
                                ```
                                
                                `path` runs from the root of the checked value to the offending field: strings for dictionary keys, integers for block indices. An empty path is the root itself.
                                
                                ## Constraints
                                
                                A type is a bare type literal: `:string`, `:integer`, `:block`, `:dictionary`, or any other Arturo type. It is optional; leave it out to accept anything.
                                
                                `opt` (also spelled `optional`) makes a field optional. Fields are required by default.
                                
                                The rules:
                                
                                | | |
                                |---|---|
                                | `min:` / `max:` | bounds a number by value, anything else by length |
                                | `between:` | a range, e.g. `2..64`, applied the same way |
                                | `in:` | membership in a block |
                                | `size:` | exact length |
                                | `matches:` | a regex the whole value must match |
                                | `of:` | element or sub-schema constraints |
                                | `example:` | a value for `example` to use; ignored when checking |
                                
                                `of:` takes a nested schema when the field is a dictionary, and a constraint block applied to each element when it is a block. Nesting goes as deep as you like.
                                
                                `matches:` takes a regex, written out or named:
                                
                                ```red
                                handle: {/[a-z][a-z0-9-]*/}
                                account: #[ user: [:string matches: handle] ]
                                
                                check account #[user: "9lives"]
                                ; => [#[path: ["user"] code: 'pattern expected: "handle" got: "9lives"]]
                                ```
                                
                                The pattern must cover the **whole** value, not merely occur somewhere inside it. Arturo's own `match?` is a search, so `[a-z]+` alone would accept `"abc123"`. Anchors are therefore optional, and harmless if you prefer them. Name your patterns: the name is what shows up in the fault, and `should match handle` reads better than the expression echoed back.
                                
                                Declaring `matches:` on a field typed as anything but `:string` is a schema mistake, and reported once at compile time rather than once per value.
                                
                                ## Coercion
                                
                                `check` is strict: a field typed `:integer` holding `"42"` is a fault. `conform` parses first and judges the result, which suits values arriving as text: CLI arguments, query strings, CSV rows, environment variables.
                                
                                ```red
                                settings: #[
                                    port: [:integer between: 1024..65535]
                                    role: [:string default: "viewer"]
                                ]
                                
                                conform settings #[port: "8080"]
                                ; => #[port: 8080  role: "viewer"]
                                
                                conform settings #[port: "80"]
                                ; => null
                                ```
                                
                                `coerce` is the transform on its own. It never validates, so it always returns something, which is also how you get the reasons behind a `null`:
                                
                                ```red
                                check schema coerce schema value
                                ```
                                
                                Only text is parsed, and only into scalars: `:integer`, `:floating` and `:logical` from strings that spell them unambiguously, plus `:integer` widening to `:floating`. Nothing is ever coerced *into* a string. `to :string` succeeds on anything, so allowing that direction would launder every type error in the schema into a pass. Anything that can't be converted is passed through untouched for `check` to judge.
                                
                                `default:` fills a field that isn't there, and only ever in `coerce` and `conform`. `check` reports on the value as given, so a field with a default is still `missing` to `check`. A default is something you asked for, not something the data claimed.
                                
                                Both functions return a copy. Dictionaries alias on assignment in Arturo, so the value you pass in is left unmodified at any depth.
                                
                                ## Generating values
                                
                                A schema read forwards is a validator. Read backwards it is a generator, which makes it its own test fixture:
                                
                                ```red
                                example account
                                ; => #[name: "abc"  port: 1024  role: "admin"  tags: ["abc" "abc" "abc"]]
                                ```
                                
                                `example` is deterministic, so you can assert against it. `example.random` varies instead, which turns a schema into a fuzzer for the checker. The property under test is that every example satisfies the schema it came from:
                                
                                ```red
                                loop 1..200 'i [
                                    v: example.random account
                                    unless empty? check account v -> print ["counterexample:" v]
                                ]
                                ```
                                
                                It honours value and length bounds, `in:` sets, exact sizes, nested schemas and block elements. In random mode optional fields are sometimes absent, since that is a distinct shape of value worth generating.
                                
                                It cannot invent two things. A regex does not run backwards, and an exotic type is a gap in the generator rather than in the schema. Both come out as `null`, which `check` then reports. The fix in either case is `example:`, a value the generator uses verbatim:
                                
                                ```red
                                #[ handle: [:string matches: handleRule example: "lulzx"] ]
                                ```
                                
                                `example:` is ignored when checking; it exists only to be read backwards.
                                
                                The function and the rule share a name because they share an idea: a value the schema accepts. `example:` states one the generator could not have invented, and `example` derives the rest. Two words for that would have been two words for the same thing.
                                
                                Not `sample`, which is already an Arturo builtin, and this package splices into the caller's scope. Taking that name would break `sample [1 2 3]` for anyone importing it.
                                
                                That reasoning covers the whole package, not just the one name. `import "schema"!` splices exactly six words worth having -- `check`, `check?`, `coerce`, `conform`, `example` and `explain` -- and every internal is prefixed `esc` so that nothing else you import, or name yourself, collides with it. `describe` in particular belongs to [unitt](https://github.com/RickBarretto/unitt); schema's own fault renderer is `schDescribe`.
                                
                                ## Design
                                
                                Fields are required by default. A schema states what is allowed, so silence means "not allowed". The same reasoning makes the check closed by default: a key the schema does not mention is a fault rather than a shrug, and `.open` is the escape hatch.
                                
                                Every fault is collected. Sibling fields are checked independently, so one bad field never hides another. The walk stops only at a value that fails its *type* check, where there is nothing left to descend into and reporting phantom missing fields underneath would be noise.
                                
                                `check` mutates nothing and throws nothing, including on a malformed schema, a value no rule can measure, or a schema that is not a dictionary at all. A bad constraint surfaces as a `badSchema` fault at the field's own path rather than an exception from somewhere in the walker, and a rule that cannot hold -- `min:` against a logical, `size:` against a number -- is reported as the ordinary fault it is. `example` reads the same diagnostics: a schema it cannot run backwards yields null rather than a guess.
                                
                                Validation and coercion are separate passes. `check` answers a question and changes nothing; `coerce` changes things and answers nothing. Keeping them apart lets you run `check` against a schema you don't trust, and makes the coercion rules auditable on their own. `conform` is the two composed.
                                
                                A constraint block is a value, so reuse is ordinary assignment:
                                
                                ```red
                                shortName: [:string min: 2 max: 8]
                                person: #[ first: shortName  last: shortName ]
                                ```
                                
                                ## Working with inert blocks
                                
                                Constraint blocks are never evaluated, which is what makes them inspectable, but it means anything with its own syntax arrives as loose tokens. `schema` folds the shapes that matter back into real values, so they read the way they do in ordinary Arturo:
                                
                                - `between: 2..64` arrives as `2`, `..`, `64` and is rebuilt into a range
                                - `of: #[city: [:string]]` arrives as `#` followed by a block and is rebuilt into a dictionary
                                - `of: address` arrives as the *word* `address` and is resolved to the schema it names
                                - `default: false` arrives as the *word* `false` and is folded back into a logical, inside blocks as well, so `in: [true false]` reads the way it looks
                                
                                Regex literals need no such handling. They survive a constraint block intact.
                                
                                `.open` is read with `attr` rather than `attr?`. In 0.10.0 a refinement seen by `attr?` stays visible to every later call in the process, so a single `check.open` made every subsequent `check` open too. A regression test covers it.
                                
                                ## Status
                                
                                Types, presence, all the rules, nesting, paths, schema diagnostics, coercion, defaults and generation are implemented. The suite covers them, including a differential pass that fuzzes the checker against its own generator, and a sweep of every rule against every shape of value that holds the walker to its promise of throwing nothing. No dependencies beyond Arturo itself.
                                
                                ## Tests
                                
                                The suite runs on [unitt](https://github.com/RickBarretto/unitt):
                                
                                ```sh
                                arturo -p install unitt
                                ~/.arturo/packages/bin/unitt
                                ```
                                
                                Or run the examples directly:
                                
                                ```sh
                                arturo examples/basic.art
                                arturo examples/conform.art
                                arturo examples/fuzz.art
                                ```
                                
                                ## License
                                
                                MIT
                                
                        }
                        license: #[
                                name: "MIT"
                                url: "https://choosealicense.com/licenses/mit/"
                        ]
                ]
                url: "https://api.github.com/repos/Lulzx/schema.art/zipball/v0.3.0"
